# Human vs AI documentation

## Surfaces

| Surface | Audience | Role |
|---------|----------|------|
| `README.md` (any, except depth in `docs/`) | Humans | What / why / who / how to use / contribute |
| [`AGENTS.md`](../../AGENTS.md) | Humans + AI | Product intent, architecture, commands, CI, guardrails |
| `docs/**` | Implementers | Deep technical reference (ABI, CI, DSFD, logging) |

## Before rewriting product or marketing prose

Read **[AGENTS.md → Product intent](../../AGENTS.md)** for ambition, goals, comparison findings, the locked human pitch, and the **Intentionally omitted from READMEs** list. Do not invent a new pitch or reintroduce excluded topics.

## README rules

- Lead with outcome language (storage handles + content-based file intelligence).
- Scope package READMEs to that package; link root / `docs/` for workspace depth.
- Agents **may** edit READMEs when the human asks for documentation work — follow human-facing guidelines.
- Do **not** dump architecture essays, CI maps, implementation logs, or creation history into READMEs.

## AGENTS.md rules

- Durable product memory and operator/dev context live here (MindVault is not used for this repo).
- No scratchpad / change ledger; keep structured sections that stay accurate.
- Do not paste design lineage (“inspired by Windows.Storage”, UWP war stories) into READMEs.

## Forbidden blog tactics

- No invisible HTML “AI guardrail” comments in READMEs.
- No absolute “never edit README” ban — edit when asked, keep human tone.
